  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-random-measure-and-locally-finite-point-process": [
      "## Definition\n\nFix a locally compact, second-countable Hausdorff topological space $S$\n([[def-locally-compact-space]], [[def-second-countable-space]],\n[[def-hausdorff-space]]). Here locally compact means that every point has a\ncompact neighbourhood; second countable means that the topology has an at most\ncountable basis; and Hausdorff means that distinct points have disjoint open\nneighbourhoods. Let $\\mathcal B(S)$ be the Borel sigma-algebra generated by the\nopen sets ([[def-borel-sigma-algebra]]).\n\nA **counting measure** on $S$ is a Borel measure of the form\n$\\mu=\\sum_{j\\in J}\\delta_{x_j}$ for a finite or countable index set $J$ and\npoints $x_j\\in S$, where repetitions are allowed and the empty sum is zero.\nEach $\\delta_x$ is the unit point mass at $x$\n([[def-dirac-measure]], [[prop-dirac-measure-is-a-probability-measure]]), and\nthe countable sum is a measure by\n[[thm-nonnegative-weighted-sums-of-measures]]. The **multiplicity** at $x$ is\n$\\mu(\\{x\\})$. The counting measure is **locally finite** if\n$\\mu(K)<\\infty$ for every compact subset $K\\subseteq S$\n([[def-compact-space]]).\n\nLet $\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$ be the set of locally finite counting\nmeasures, equipped with the sigma-algebra generated by the **evaluation maps**\n$\\mu\\mapsto\\mu(B)$ for $B\\in\\mathcal B(S)$. A **point process** on $S$ is a\nmeasurable map $\\eta:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to\n(\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S),\\mathcal E)$ from a probability space\n([[def-probability-measure]]), with measurability in the sense of\n[[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]]. Equivalently, every\ncount $\\eta(B)$ is a measurable random variable, because the evaluation maps\ngenerate $\\mathcal E$. Its **law** is the probability measure\n$\\mathbb P_\\eta(A)=\\mathbb P(\\eta^{-1}(A))$ on $(\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S),\\mathcal E)$\n([[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]],\n[[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]]). The process is\nclassified by its realized configurations: a configuration $\\mu$ is\n**simple** if $\\mu(\\{x\\})\\le1$ for every $x$, and is **multiplicity-bearing**\nif some singleton has mass greater than one. This is a pointwise distinction;\nno almost-sure simplicity assertion is part of this definition.\n\nLet $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$ be the set of all locally finite Borel\nmeasures on $S$, with the sigma-algebra generated by their evaluation maps. A\n**random locally finite measure** $\\xi$ is a measurable map into this space.\n\nFor a nonnegative Borel function $f$, write\n$\\eta(f):=\\int_S f\\,d\\eta$. Define the expected measure, or **intensity\nmeasure**, by $\\nu(B):=\\mathbb E[\\eta(B)]$. The expected-measure and\nrandom-integral measurability assertions are justified by\n[[lem-mean-measure-and-integrals-of-a-random-point-process]]. The measure\n$\\nu$ need not be locally finite; it is locally finite exactly when\n$\\mathbb E[\\eta(K)]<\\infty$ for each compact $K$.\n\nThe mean measure of a random locally finite measure is likewise\n$\\nu(B)=\\mathbb E[\\xi(B)]$, covered by the same expected-measure lemma. Local\nfiniteness of each realization does not by itself assert local finiteness of\n$\\nu$."
    ],
    "lem-locally-compact-second-countable-spaces-have-compact-exhaustions": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $S$ be locally compact, second countable, and Hausdorff. There is an\nincreasing sequence of compact subsets $K_n$ such that $\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}K_n=S$\nand every compact subset of $S$ is contained in some $K_n$."
    ],
    "def-scalar-poisson-count-law": [
      "## Definition\n\nFor a finite parameter $\\lambda\\ge0$, the **Poisson count law**\n$\\operatorname{Pois}(\\lambda)$ is the probability measure on\n$\\mathbb N_0=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$ whose masses are\n$$\\operatorname{Pois}(\\lambda)\\{k\\}=e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!},\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb N_0,$$\nwhen $\\lambda>0$, and is the point mass at $0$ when $\\lambda=0$. The\nnormalization and the moment and generating-function identities used below are\nproved in [[lem-poisson-count-law-normalization-and-moments]]. The separate\nextended convention for a point-process count on a Borel set of infinite\nintensity is that the count equals $+\\infty$ almost surely; it is not a finite\nPoisson probability law."
    ],
    "lem-mean-measure-and-integrals-of-a-random-point-process": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\zeta$ be a random locally finite Borel measure on $S$; in particular,\n$\\zeta$ may be a point process. Then $\\nu(B):=\\mathbb E[\\zeta(B)]$ is a\nmeasure, and for every nonnegative Borel $f$ the map\n$\\omega\\mapsto\\zeta_\\omega(f)$ is a measurable extended nonnegative random\nvariable satisfying $\\mathbb E[\\zeta(f)]=\\int f\\,d\\nu$. The mean measure\n$\\nu$ is locally finite if and only if\n$\\mathbb E[\\zeta(K)]<\\infty$ for every compact $K$."
    ],
    "lem-point-process-law-is-determined-by-count-fidi": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\eta$ and $\\eta'$ be point processes on $S$ with the evaluation sigma\nalgebra on $\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$. If every finite vector of Borel-set\ncounts has the same distribution under $\\eta$ and $\\eta'$, then their laws on\n$\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$ are equal."
    ],
    "lem-poisson-count-law-normalization-and-moments": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor finite $\\lambda\\ge0$, the masses in\n[[def-scalar-poisson-count-law]] sum to one. If $N\\sim\\operatorname{Pois}(\\lambda)$,\nthen\n$$\\mathbb E[N]=\\lambda,\\qquad \\mathbb E[N(N-1)]=\\lambda^2,\\qquad \\mathbb E[N^2]=\\lambda^2+\\lambda,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(N)=\\lambda,$$\nand $\\mathbb E[s^N]=\\exp(\\lambda(s-1))$ for $0\\le s\\le1$."
    ],
    "lem-deletion-of-one-point-is-measurable": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet\n$$D:=\\{(x,\\mu)\\in S\\times\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S):\\mu(\\{x\\})\\ge1\\}.$$\nThen $D$ is measurable in the product evaluation sigma-algebra, and the map\n$R:D\\to\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$ defined by $R(x,\\mu)=\\mu-\\delta_x$ is\nmeasurable. The subtraction removes one occurrence, so any other occurrences\nat $x$ remain."
    ],
    "def-poisson-and-cox-point-process": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $S$ be as in [[def-random-measure-and-locally-finite-point-process]]. A\n**Poisson point process with locally finite, sigma-finite intensity measure\n$\\nu$** is a point process $\\eta$ such that for every finite pairwise-disjoint\nfamily of Borel sets $B_1,\\ldots,B_m$, the counts $\\eta(B_i)$ are independent\nrandom elements ([[def-independent-random-elements]]) and $\\eta(B_i)$ has the\nscalar Poisson law of mean $\\nu(B_i)$\n([[def-scalar-poisson-count-law]],\n[[lem-poisson-count-law-normalization-and-moments]]). If $\\nu(B)=\\infty$,\nthe extended count convention is $\\eta(B)=+\\infty$ almost surely.\nHere sigma-finite means that $S$ is a countable union of Borel sets of finite\n$\\nu$-measure ([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]); for the\nstated $S$, local finiteness also implies sigma-finiteness by the compact\nexhaustion in\n[[lem-locally-compact-second-countable-spaces-have-compact-exhaustions]].\n\nLet $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$ be the evaluation-measurable space of locally\nfinite Borel measures. A **Cox point process directed by a random measure\n$\\xi$** consists of a point process $\\eta$, a random locally finite measure\n$\\xi$, and a supplied probability kernel $K$ from $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$\nto $\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$ such that $K(\\xi,\\cdot)$ is a regular\nconditional law of $\\eta$ given $\\xi$\n([[def-conditional-law-given-a-random-element]]) and, for $P_\\xi$-almost every\n$\\mu$, $K(\\mu,\\cdot)$ is the law of a Poisson point process of intensity\n$\\mu$. The kernel is part of the model data; this definition does not assert\nthat such a kernel exists for every $(\\eta,\\xi)$. The mean measure of $\\xi$ is\n$\\nu(B)=\\mathbb E[\\xi(B)]$, a measure by\n[[lem-mean-measure-and-integrals-of-a-random-point-process]].\n\nBoth definitions permit multiplicities. They do not assert that diffuse\ndirecting measures yield simple processes or that a Cox law determines the law\nof its directing measure."
    ],
    "thm-finite-intensity-poisson-process-existence-and-mixed-binomial-law": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\nu$ be a finite Borel measure on $S$\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]) and put $\\lambda=\\nu(S)$. A\nPoisson point process of intensity $\\nu$ exists. If $\\lambda=0$ it is the zero\nprocess. If $\\lambda>0$, its law is the push-forward of\n$$\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^n}{n!}\\left(\\frac{\\nu}{\\lambda}\\right)^{\\otimes n}$$\non the disjoint union $\\coprod_{n\\ge0}S^n$ under\n$(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\mapsto\\sum_{i=1}^n\\delta_{x_i}$. Every Poisson point\nprocess of finite total intensity has this same law. This is the\n**mixed-binomial representation**: the total count is Poisson and, in the\nconstruction, the $n$ locations in component $S^n$ have product law\n$(\\nu/\\lambda)^{\\otimes n}$."
    ],
    "lem-poisson-integral-moment-identities": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\eta$ be a Poisson point process with locally finite intensity measure\n$\\nu$. For every nonnegative Borel function $f$,\n$$\\mathbb E[\\eta(f)]=\\nu(f),\\qquad \\mathbb E[\\eta(f)^2]=\\nu(f^2)+\\nu(f)^2,$$\nas identities in $[0,\\infty]$. Here $\\eta(f)=\\int f\\,d\\eta$ and\n$\\nu(f)=\\int f\\,d\\nu$."
    ],
    "lem-finite-intensity-mecke-identity": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\eta$ be a Poisson point process on $S$ with finite intensity measure\n$\\nu$ (so $\\nu(S)<\\infty$ under\n[[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]), and let\n$h:S\\times\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)\\to[0,\\infty]$ be\nmeasurable. Addition $(x,\\mu)\\mapsto\\mu+\\delta_x$ is interpreted as addition\nof counting measures. Then\n$$\\mathbb E\\left[\\int_S h(x,\\eta)\\,\\eta(dx)\\right]=\\int_S\\mathbb E[h(x,\\eta+\\delta_x)]\\,\\nu(dx).$$\nBoth sides are well-defined extended nonnegative integrals."
    ],
    "thm-poisson-point-process-existence": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume countable choice and dependent choice. Let $S$ be locally compact,\nsecond countable, and Hausdorff, and let $\\nu$ be a locally finite,\nsigma-finite Borel measure on $S$. Then a locally finite Poisson point\nprocess with intensity $\\nu$ exists. This existence assertion uses countable\nchoice and dependent choice only through the countable product of probability\nspaces; it does not assume full choice."
    ],
    "thm-campbell-laplace-mecke-and-cox-moments": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\eta$ be a Poisson point process on the locally compact, second-countable Hausdorff space $S$, with locally finite, sigma-finite intensity measure $\\nu$. For every nonnegative Borel $f$, its Campbell and Laplace formulas are $\\mathbb E[\\eta(f)]=\\nu(f)$ and $\\mathbb E[e^{-\\eta(f)}]=e^{-\\nu(1-e^{-f})}$, with $e^{-\\infty}=0$. For every nonnegative $g$ measurable for the product of the Borel and evaluation sigma-algebras on $S\\times\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{lf}}(S)$, the add-one Mecke formula and its deletion form are $\\mathbb E[\\int g(x,\\eta)\\,\\eta(dx)]=\\int\\mathbb E[g(x,\\eta+\\delta_x)]\\,\\nu(dx)$ and $\\mathbb E[\\int g(x,\\eta-\\delta_x)\\,\\eta(dx)]=\\int\\mathbb E[g(x,\\eta)]\\,\\nu(dx)$; in the second integral one occurrence is deleted at each counted point, with multiplicity retained. Conversely, a locally finite point process with locally finite, sigma-finite mean measure $\\nu$ is Poisson if it satisfies the deletion identity for every such $g$.\n\nIf $\\eta$ is Cox directed by a random locally finite measure $\\xi$ with supplied probability kernel $K$, write $P_\\xi$ for the law of $\\xi$ and $\\nu(B)=\\mathbb E\\xi(B)$ for its mean measure. Then for $P_\\xi$-almost every $\\mu$, $\\int e^{-\\zeta(f)}K(\\mu,d\\zeta)=e^{-\\mu(1-e^{-f})}$ and $\\int\\zeta(f)^2K(\\mu,d\\zeta)=\\mu(f^2)+\\mu(f)^2$. Consequently $\\mathbb E\\eta(f)=\\nu(f)$, $\\mathbb E[e^{-\\eta(f)}]=\\mathbb E[e^{-\\xi(1-e^{-f})}]$, and $\\mathbb E[\\eta(f)^2]=\\nu(f^2)+\\mathbb E[\\xi(f)^2]$, all as extended nonnegative identities. Thus $\\mathbb E[\\eta(f)^2]<\\infty$ exactly when $\\int f^2\\,d\\nu<\\infty$ and $\\mathbb E[\\xi(f)^2]<\\infty$; then $\\operatorname{Var}(\\eta(f))=\\nu(f^2)+\\operatorname{Var}(\\xi(f))$. All formulas allow multiplicities and assert neither simplicity under diffuse intensity nor uniqueness of a Cox directing law."
    ],
    "ex-poisson-and-cox-count-laws-on-a-finite-window": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $W$ be a compact Borel window in a locally compact, second-countable\nHausdorff space $S$, and let $\\mu$ be a finite Borel measure\n([[def-finite-sigma-finite-and-semifinite-measures]]) supported on $W$ with\n$\\mu(W)=1$. A Poisson point process of intensity $\\mu$ and the Cox\nprocess directed by $\\xi=Z\\mu$, where $\\mathbb P(Z=0)=\\mathbb P(Z=2)=1/2$,\nboth have mean count one in $W$. Their count variances differ: the Poisson\nvariance is $1$, whereas the Cox variance is $2$."
    ]
  }
}
